L000585	\section{Velocity of the soliton}\label{app:Velocity}
L000586	In this section, we derive the dependence of the spinor phase-kink velocity on the quadratic Zeeman shift $q$ as given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:velocity} in the main text. To this end, we first derive the spin-nematic continuity equation of the spin-1 Bose gas \cite{Yu2022}. We use this to calculate the velocity dependence of the spinor phase solitons on the quadratic Zeeman shift $q$.
L000587	
L000588	
L000589	
L000590	
L000591	\subsection{Spin-nematic continuity equation}
L000592	We begin by calculating the time derivative of the magnetization density
L000593	
L000594	\begin{align}
L000595	    \partial_t F_i = \partial_t \left( \boldsymbol{\psi}^\dagger f_i \boldsymbol{\psi} \right)
L000596	    = (\partial_t \boldsymbol{\psi}^\dagger) f_i \boldsymbol{\psi} + \boldsymbol{\psi}^\dagger f_i (\partial_t \boldsymbol{\psi}),
L000597	    \label{eq:spin_derivative}
L000598	\end{align}
L000599	
L000600	which is the observable of interest.
L000601	Next, we define the differential operator given by the one-dimensional GPE as
L000602	
L000603	\begin{align}
L000604	    \mathcal{D}_\mathrm{GPE} = &-\frac{\hbar^2}{2M}\partial_x^2+V(x)+pf_z+qf_z^2  \\&+\frac{1}{2}c_0|\boldsymbol{\psi}(x,t)|^2+\frac{1}{2}c_1\boldsymbol{F}(x,t)\boldsymbol{f}\, , \nonumber
L000605	\end{align}
L000606	
L000607	and write the GPE and its Hermitian conjugate in compact notation,
L000608	
L000609	\begin{align}
L000610	    \partial_t \boldsymbol{\psi} = -\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} \mathcal{D}_\mathrm{GPE}\boldsymbol{\psi} \, , \quad
L000611	    \partial_t \boldsymbol{\psi}^\dagger = \frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} \boldsymbol{\psi}^\dagger \mathcal{D}_\mathrm{GPE} \, ,
L000612	\end{align}
L000613	
L000614	where, for brevity, the spatial derivatives in $\mathcal{D}_\mathrm{GPE}$ act to the left in the Hermitian conjugate case.
L000615	Hence, we can write Eq. \eqref{eq:spin_derivative} as
L000616	
L000617	\begin{align}
L000618	    \partial_t F_i = \frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger} \left[\mathcal{D}_\mathrm{GPE}, f_i\right] \boldsymbol{\psi}.
L000619	    \label{eq:spin_evolution}
L000620	\end{align}
L000621	The kinetic term in Eq.~\eqref{eq:spin_evolution} can be written in the form of a spin current term
L000622	
L000623	\begin{align}
L000624	    {J}^\mathrm{F}_i = \frac{\hbar}{2M \mathrm{i}}\left[ \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger} f_i \partial_x \boldsymbol{\psi} - (\partial_x \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger}) f_i\boldsymbol{\psi}  \right] \, .
L000625	\end{align}
L000626	
L000627	The commutator of the density interaction term in Eq.~\eqref{eq:spin_evolution} vanishes. Therefore, we write the time evolution of the spin density with the remaining terms containing spin matrices as
L000628	
L000629	\begin{align}
L000630	     \partial_t F_i &=  -\partial_x {J}^\mathrm{F}_i \nonumber \\ &+ \frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger} \left[pf_z+qf_z^2+\frac{1}{2}c_1\boldsymbol{F}(x,t)\boldsymbol{f}, f_i\right] \boldsymbol{\psi}.
L000631	\end{align}
L000632	
L000633	The next step involves calculating the commutators arising from the spin interactions and the linear and quadratic Zeeman shifts using the $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ commutation relations \\
L000634	
L000635	\begin{align}
L000636	    \left[\frac{c_1}{2}F_j f_j, f_i\right] &= \mathrm{i} \frac{c_1}{2}F_j  \epsilon_{jik} f_k\, , \\[0.2cm]
L000637	    p \, [f_z, f_i] &=  \mathrm{i} \, p \epsilon_{zik} f_k, \, \\[0.25cm]
L000638	    q \; [f_zf_z, f_i] &= \mathrm{i} \, q \epsilon_{zik} \{f_z, f_k\}\, , \label{eq:Nematic_qpart}
L000639	\end{align}
L000640	
L000641	where $\epsilon_{ijk}$ is the three-dimensional antisymmetric Levi-Civita symbol.
L000642	
L000643	Collecting all terms, we arrive at
L000644	
L000645	\begin{align}
L000646	    \partial_t F_i = &-\partial_x {J}^\mathrm{F}_i
L000647	    - \frac{1}{\hbar}\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger}\Big(\frac{c_1}{2}F_j \epsilon_{jik} f_k  \nonumber \\[0.25cm]
L000648	    &\hspace*{1.5cm}+ p \epsilon_{zik} f_k + q\epsilon_{zik}\{f_z, f_k\}\Big)\boldsymbol{\psi}. \label{eq:spin_evolution_anticomm}
L000649	\end{align}
L000650	
L000651	
L000652	The last term in the first line of Eq.~\eqref{eq:spin_evolution_anticomm} vanishes due to the anti-symmetry of the Levi-Civita symbol. The term proportional to $q$ can be written as a nematic source term
L000653	
L000654	$K_{iz} = \sum_k \epsilon_{izk} \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger} Q_{zk} \boldsymbol{\psi}$
L000655	
L000656	in terms of the normalized nematic operator
L000657	
L000658	$Q_{ij} = (f_if_j + f_jf_i)/2$,
L000659	
L000660	ultimately leading to the spin-nematic continuity equation
L000661	\begin{align}
L000662	    \frac{\partial}{\partial t} F_i + \partial_x {J}_i^\mathrm{F} = \frac{1}{\hbar} \left(2qK_{iz} + p\epsilon_{zik} F_k \right)\, .
L000663	    \label{eq:Spin-Nematic-continuity}
L000664	\end{align}
L000665	
L000666	
L000667	
L000668	
L000669	
L000670	\subsection{Spinor-phase soliton velocity from spin-continuity equation}
L000671	In the following, we derive the dependence of the spinor-phase kink from the spin nematic continuity equation~\eqref{eq:Spin-Nematic-continuity}.
L000672	The linear Zeeman shift in the spin-1 Hamiltonian can be absorbed into the fields by considering a rotating frame of reference, hence setting $p=0$. Next, we consider the core of the magnetic soliton, where the magnetization vanishes, leading to a net zero current ${J}_i^\mathrm{F} = 0$. However, its spatial derivative
L000673	
L000674	$\partial_x{J}_i^\mathrm{F}$
L000675	
L000676	does not necessarily vanish.
L000677	We consider a sine-Gordon kink solution in the spinor phase,
L000678	
L000679	\begin{align}
L000680	    \psi_{0} = \sqrt{n_{0}}\e^{- \frac{\mathrm{i}}{2} \varphi_{\mathrm{S},\mathrm{K}}},
L000681	    \quad \psi_{\pm1} = \sqrt{n_{\pm1}}\e^{\frac{\mathrm{i}}{2} \varphi_{\mathrm{S},\mathrm{K}}},
L000682	    \label{eq:hydro_parametrization_spinor}
L000683	\end{align}
L000684	
L000685	with
L000686	
L000687	\begin{align}
L000688	    \varphi_{\mathrm{S},\mathrm{K}}(x) = 2 \arctan{(\e^{x/\ell})}, \label{eq:BlakieLösung}
L000689	\end{align}
L000690	
L000691	and $\ell = \hbar/\sqrt{4|c_1|Mn}$ as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:kink_analytics}. We remark that this class of solutions was shown to solve the spin-1 GPE in the easy-plane phase \cite{Yu2021}. Generally, the following calculation holds for any phase profile, since we evaluate the final solution at the core of the soliton. In particular, it holds for the anti-kink $\overline{K}$ as well, but with a relative factor of $(-1)$ in $\phis$.
L000692	We boost the stationary solution $x \mapsto x - vt$ and obtain a time-dependent solution with velocity $v$. Hence, the time derivative of the phase factors in Eq.~\eqref{eq:hydro_parametrization_spinor} reads
L000693	
L000694	\begin{align}
L000695	    \frac{\partial }{\partial t} \e^{\pm \mathrm{i} \varphi_{\mathrm{S}, \mathrm{K}}(x -vt)} &= \mp 2\mathrm{i} \frac{v}{\ell} \frac{\e^{(x-vt)/\ell}}{\e^{2(x-vt)/\ell} + 1} \e^{\pm \mathrm{i} \varphi_{\mathrm{S}, \mathrm{K}}(x-vt)} \, .
L000696	\label{eq:der_arctan}
L000697	\end{align}
L000698	
L000699	Notice that we neglect a factor of one half in the exponent, since it is missing in the phase factors of the observable $F_x$ as well.
L000700	We evaluate the expression at the core of the soliton, $x-vt=0$,
L000701	which yields
L000702	
L000703	\begin{align}
L000704	     \left.\frac{\partial }{\partial t} \e^{\pm \mathrm{i} \varphi_{\mathrm{S}, \mathrm{K}}(x-vt)}\right|_{x=vt}=  \pm \frac{v}{\ell} .
L000705	\end{align}
L000706	
L000707	Considering the spin in the $x$ direction, given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:Spins}, we compute the time evolution of the spin, i.e., the first part of the left-hand side of the continuity equation~\eqref{eq:Spin-Nematic-continuity}
L000708	
L000709	\begin{align}
L000710	    \frac{\partial}{\partial t} F_x =
L000711	    &\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \left[\sqrt{n_{-1}n_0}\e^{-\mathrm{i} \varphi_{\mathrm{S},\mathrm{K}}}\right. \nonumber \\
L000712	    &+ \left.\sqrt{n_0}(\sqrt{n_{+1}}+ \sqrt{n_{-1}})\e^{\mathrm{i} \varphi_{\mathrm{S},\mathrm{K}}} + \sqrt{n_{+1}n_0} \e^{-\mathrm{i} \varphi_{\mathrm{S},\mathrm{K}}} \right] \nonumber \\
L000713	    = &\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\frac{v}{l} \left[\mathrm{i} \; \psi^*_{-1}\psi_0 -\mathrm{i} \; \psi_0^*(\psi_{-1} + \psi_1) +\mathrm{i} \;\psi^*_{1}\psi_0\right] \\
L000714	    = &-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \frac{v}{\ell}\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger}
L000715	     \begin{bmatrix}
L000716	0 & -\mathrm{i} & 0 \\
L000717	\mathrm{i} & 0 & \mathrm{i} \\
L000718	0 & -\mathrm{i} & 0
L000719	\end{bmatrix}
L000720	\boldsymbol{\psi}.
L000721	    \label{eq:RHSconteq_filledout}
L000722	\end{align}
L000723	
L000724	Calculating the gradient of the current yields
L000725	\begin{align}
L000726	    \partial_x {J}_x^\mathrm{F} = \frac{\hbar}{2M\mathrm{i}} \left[\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger} f_x \partial_x^2 \boldsymbol{\psi} - (\partial_x^2 \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger}) f_x \boldsymbol{\psi}\right].
L000727	    \label{ed:current_dev}
L000728	\end{align}
L000729	We then proceed with calculating the second spatial derivative of the phase factor of the moving soliton solution as
L000730	\begin{align}
L000731	    \partial_x^2 &\e^{\pm i \varphi_{\mathrm{S, K}}} = \frac{1}{\ell^2}\bigg\{\Big(\pm 2\mathrm{i} \frac{\e^{(x-vt)/\ell}}{\e^{2(x-vt)/\ell}+1}\Big)^2 \nonumber \\
L000732	    &\pm 2 \mathrm{i} \; \frac{\e^{(x-vt)/\ell}-\e^{3(x-vt)/\ell} }{(\e^{2(x-vt)/\ell}+1)^2}  \bigg\}\e^{\pm \mathrm{i}\varphi_{\mathrm{S,K}}}\;.
L000733	    \label{eq:second_dev}
L000734	\end{align}
L000735	Again, evaluating Eq. \eqref{eq:second_dev} at the soliton core, $x-vt = 0$, we obtain
L000736	\begin{align}
L000737	    \left.\partial_x^2 \e^{\pm \frac{\mathrm{i}}{2} \varphi_{\mathrm{S, K}}}\right|_{x=vt} =  \mp \frac{\mathrm{i}}{2\ell^2} .
L000738	\end{align}
L000739	Plugging this into Eq. \eqref{ed:current_dev} yields
L000740	\begin{align}
L000741	   \partial_x {J}_x^\mathrm{F} =&
L000742	     \frac{\hbar}{2M \mathrm{i}} \frac{\mathrm{i}}{2\ell^2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\bigg(\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger}
L000743	     \begin{bmatrix}
L000744	0 & 1 & 0 \\
L000745	1 & 0 & 1 \\
L000746	0 & 1 & 0
L000747	\end{bmatrix}
L000748	\boldsymbol{\psi} \nonumber \\
L000749	&- \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger}
L000750	     \begin{bmatrix}
L000751	0 & 1 & 0 \\
L000752	1 & 0 & 1 \\
L000753	0 & 1 & 0
L000754	\end{bmatrix}
L000755	\boldsymbol{\psi} \bigg) = 0.
L000756	\end{align}
L000757	Both terms already equate to zero individually, since $F_x$ vanishes at the soliton core and the derivative only introduces a factor.
L000758	The right-hand side of the continuity equation reads
L000759	
L000760	\begin{align}
L000761	   \frac{2q}{\hbar} K_{xz} &= \frac{q}{\hbar} \epsilon_{xzy}  \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger} (f_zf_y + f_yf_z) \boldsymbol{\psi} \\
L000762	   &= -\frac{q}{\hbar} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \boldsymbol{\psi}^{\dagger}
L000763	   \begin{bmatrix}
L000764	0 & -\mathrm{i} & 0 \\
L000765	\mathrm{i} & 0 & \mathrm{i} \\
L000766	0 & -\mathrm{i} & 0
L000767	\end{bmatrix}
L000768	\boldsymbol{\psi}  \,. \label{eq:LHSconteq_filledout}
L000769	\end{align}
L000770	
L000771	Plugging all expressions into Eq. \eqref{eq:Spin-Nematic-continuity},
L000772	we arrive at a linear relation,
L000773	
L000774	\begin{align}
L000775	    \frac{v}{\ell} = \frac{q}{\hbar} \; ,
L000776	\end{align}
L000777	
L000778	which recovers Eq.~\eqref{eq:velocity} in the main text.
L000779	Note that a density dependence enters via the natural size of the soliton solution, which is proportional to the spin healing length, i.e.  $\ell^{-2}\sim n$.
L000780	
L000781	\subsection{Spinor-phase soliton velocity from effective sG theory\label{VelocityCalcEFT}}
L000782	In the following, we give an alternative derivation of Eq.~\eqref{eq:velocity} within the effective sG theory.
L000783	With the Lagrangian given in \cite{Siovitz2025} (where only density fluctuations for small momenta were taken into account in the derivation of the effective theory)
L000784	the double sine-Gordon equation is found to be:
L000785	\begin{align}
L000786	         0= \; &\partial^2_{\overline{t}}\phis-2(1-\overline{q}^2)\partial^2_{\overline{x}}\phis +2(1-2\overline{q}^2)\sin{(2\phis)}\nonumber \\ &+\overline{q}^2\sin{(4\phis)} \; .
L000787	\end{align}
L000788	Here $\overline{x}$ and $\overline{t}$ are the space and time coordinates in units of spin healing length and spin interaction time, and $\overline{q}$ is $q$ in units of $2n|c_1|$.
L000789	We rescale $\overline{x}$ as $\Tilde{x} = \overline{x}/\sqrt{2(1-\overline{q}^2)}$.
L000790	In the sine-Gordon limit, the solution for a single kink or anti-kink with dimensionless velocity $\overline{v}$ and offset $\Tilde{x}_0$ for this equation is:
L000791	\begin{equation}
L000792	    \phis=\pm2\arctan{\left(\exp{\sqrt{2}\sqrt{\frac{1-2\overline{q}^2}{1-\overline{q}^2}}\frac{\Tilde{x}-\overline{v} \overline{t}-\Tilde{x}_0}{\sqrt{1-\overline{v}^2}}}\right)}
L000793	\end{equation}
L000794	As we find the soliton to have a constant width of $\ell= \xi_\mathrm{s}/\sqrt{2}$, which in units of $\overline{x}$ is $\overline{\ell}=1/\sqrt{2}$, the factors in the argument of the exponential function should multiply to the inverse of $\overline{\ell}$, leading to:
L000795	\begin{equation}
L000796	    \frac{1}{\overline{\ell}} = \sqrt{2} = \sqrt{2}\sqrt{\frac{1-2\overline{q}^2}{1-\overline{q}^2}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\overline{v}^2}} \; .
L000797	\end{equation}
L000798	When solving this for the dimensionless velocity, one obtains:
L000799	\begin{equation}
L000800	    \overline{v}=\frac{\overline{q}}{\sqrt{1-\overline{q}^2}} \; .
L000801	\end{equation}
L000802	This can be transformed back to the observed velocity $v_{\mathrm{obs}}$ in physical units:
L000803	\begin{align}
L000804	    v_{\mathrm{obs}}= \; &\frac{\overline{t}}{t}\frac{x}{\overline{x}}\sqrt{2(1-\overline{q}^2)}\overline{v} \nonumber \\
L000805	    = \; &\sqrt{2}\frac{\overline{t}}{t}\frac{x}{\overline{x}}\overline{q} \nonumber \\
L000806	    = \; & \frac{\xi_\mathrm{s}}{\sqrt{2}\hbar}q \; .
L000807	\end{align}
L000808	With $\ell=\xi_\mathrm{s}/\sqrt{2}$, this gives:
L000809	\begin{equation}
L000810	    \frac{v_{\mathrm{obs}}}{\ell} = \frac{q}{\hbar} \; .
L000811	\end{equation}
L000812	We are therefore able to derive the velocity-$q$ dependence even in the effective sG theory, thereby further showing that the experimentally generated spinor-phase kink is described by an effective sG theory.
L000813	
L000814	
L000815	
L000816	
L000817	\section{Phase shift from sG theory}\label{app:PhaseShift}
L000818	
L000819	In this section, we calculate the value of the phase shift from the effective sG theory derived in \cite{Siovitz2025} in order to see if the effective theory is also able to quantitatively describe soliton collisions. We start from the equation of motion
L000820	\begin{equation}
L000821	    \partial_{\bar{t}}^2 \phis - 2(1-\bar{q}^2) \partial_{\bar{x}}^2 \phis + 4(1-2 \bar{q}^2) \mathrm{sin}(\phis) = 0 \; .
L000822	\end{equation}
L000823	To obtain an analytical estimate of the phase shift $\delta x$, we use the formula for the phase shift in the sine-Gordon model in standard form,
L000824	\begin{equation}\label{eq:AnalyticsPhaseShift}
L000825	    \delta x = \frac{2 \sqrt{1-(v/c_\mathrm{s})^2}}{m}\ln{\frac{c_\mathrm{s}}{v}},
L000826	\end{equation}
L000827	as calculated in \cite{Koch2023}, where $m=\sqrt{\lambda}$ is the mass of the sine-Gordon field.
L000828	The mass of the effective theory derived in \cite{Siovitz2025}  is given by
L000829	$m=2\sqrt{1-2\overline{q}^2}$, with $\overline{q}=q/2n|c_1|$, and an additional factor in the transformation of space to dimensionless coordinates of $1 / \sqrt{2(1-\overline{q}^2)}$.
L000830	This leads to the following relations for the phase shift $\delta\overline{x}$ in units of $\overline{x}$:
L000831	\begin{align}
L000832	    \delta\overline{x}&= \frac{\sqrt{2-2\overline{q}^2 - \overline{v}^2}}{\sqrt{1-2\overline{q}^2}}\ln{\frac{\sqrt{2(1-\overline{q}^2)}}{\overline{v}}}\:,\quad
L000833	\end{align}
L000834	To obtain the velocity $\overline{v}$ in units of $\overline{x}$ and $\overline{t}$ and then also the phase shift $\delta x$ in physical units, the relations $x=\xi_\mathrm{s}\overline{x}=\sqrt{2}\ell \overline{x}$ and $t=\frac{2M\xi_\mathrm{s}^2}{\hbar}\overline{t}=\frac{4M\ell^2}{\hbar}\overline{t}$ \cite{Siovitz2025} are used.
L000835	The width of the collision data in the experiment was found as the mean of the fit results of the soliton widths as $\ell=5.57 \;\text{µm}$.
L000836	With an incoming velocity obtained from the fit as $v_{\mathrm{in}}=16.76\; \text{µm}/\text{s}$ while keeping the aforementioned approximation $\bar{q}=0$, this gives $\delta x = 14.30 \;\text{µm}$. \\
L000837	This value is more than twice as large as the measured (and numerical) value for the phase shift. This is, however, not surprising as the assumption of constant densities $n_{\mf}$ made in the derivation of the effective sG theory in \cite{Siovitz2025} is violated during collision. We therefore have fitted Eq.~\eqref{eq:AnalyticsPhaseShift} to phase shifts extracted from numerical spin-1 simulation, see Fig.~\ref{fig:numerical_phase_shift},  and found that with a different sG mass of $m_{\mathrm{fit}} = 2.53\pm0.02$ we can still capture the behavior during collision in an effective sG theory.
